cfgs={18:dict(title='When Threat Persists: Stress, Anxiety, and Recovery',booktitle='Stress and Anxiety Disorders',theme='storm-blue-paper',color='354D65',accent='627B94',pages='580–603',pdfpages='60–83',image='title_adrenal',credit='De Agostini Picture Library/Science Source',filename='Stress_and_Anxiety_Disorders_Chapter_18.pptx',takeaways=[('Stress responses have distinct routes.','Sympathetic nerves, adrenal catecholamines, and the HPA axis operate through different structures and time courses; negative feedback helps limit glucocorticoid exposure.'),('Context changes defensive action.','The same learned danger can produce freezing, retreat, flight, or social avoidance; score the actual action and identify the setting before assigning a mechanism.'),('Vulnerability and exposure are separate stages.','Development, pre-existing anatomy, and later experience can each influence persistent symptoms; an anatomical difference measured afterward does not identify when it began.'),('Extinction is new learning.','Safe experience changes the expression of a learned prediction without necessarily deleting the original association; context and prior training affect later retrieval.'),('Compulsions involve specific maintaining processes.','Relief can reinforce rituals, familiar actions can acquire value, and cell-specific striatal changes can promote repetition; ordinary grooming is not itself OCD.'),('Treatments act at different biological levels.','ERP changes learned expectations and ritual responding, reuptake inhibitors alter serotonin clearance, and stimulation changes network activity; evaluate clinical symptoms and functioning separately from animal circuit outcomes.')]),19:dict(title='From Reward to Compulsion: Drugs, Learning, and Recovery',booktitle='Substance Abuse',theme='clay-red-paper',color='69464A',accent='916B70',pages='604–635',pdfpages='84–115',image='title_cb1',credit='BSIP/Newscom',filename='Substance_Use_and_Recovery_Chapter_19.pptx',takeaways=[('Reinforcement depends on the consequence.','Obtaining reward and removing withdrawal can both strengthen use; punishment, negative reinforcement, tolerance, dependence, and impaired control are different concepts.'),('Drug mechanisms differ before they converge.','Opioids, stimulants, nicotine, alcohol, and cannabinoids begin at different receptors, transporters, or channels even when several influence dopamine-related learning.'),('Cues retain learned value.','Places, odors, internal sensations, and stress can renew responding after the drug is absent; intoxication, withdrawal, and cue-triggered seeking require separate measurements.'),('Receptor location changes exposure and signaling.','Membrane entry, phosphorylation, endocytosis, recycling, and intracellular compartments determine which targets a drug reaches and how their responses change over time.'),('A task result requires its actual preparation.','Calcium fluorescence, movement, receptor labeling, self-administration, and clinical recovery measure different events; a cellular or animal finding is not by itself a clinical treatment outcome.'),('Recovery addresses several maintaining processes.','Maintenance medicines stabilize opioid exposure, rescue antagonists reverse acute toxicity, cessation and alcohol medicines target distinct mechanisms, and behavioral care changes cues, consequences, and daily functioning.')])}
